.TH zedit "" "Command"
.SH NAME
zedit \- GUI text editor
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBzedit [\fIoptions\fB] [\fIfile\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
zedit -- GUI text editor
Usage:	zedit [ options ] [ file ]
Keys:	^B/^F/^P/^N move, ^A/^E line start/end, ^Z/^V page up/down
	^K kill line, ^W kill selection, ^Y yank, ^S find, ^X^C quit
	F2 save, F3 open, F4 new, F5 cut, F6 copy, F7 paste, F8 find
.HE
.B zedit
is the text editor of the
.SM COHERENT
GUI.
It must be run under the
.B zview
desktop; started elsewhere it exits immediately.
It edits one plain-text file in a resizable window with a scrollbar on
the left edge and a status line showing the file name, a
.B *
when the buffer is modified, and the cursor position as
.IR line , column .
.PP
With a
.I file
argument
.B zedit
loads that file; if it does not exist yet, editing starts in an empty
buffer under that name.
With no argument the buffer starts empty and untitled.
.B zedit
also accepts the standard window
.I options
of GUI applications
(\fB\-T \-I \-S \-P \-H\fR); see
.BR zview .
.PP
The keyboard commands follow the MicroEMACS convention of control keys
and prefix chords.
The C900 cursor keypad works as expected: the desktop translates the
arrow keys and the keypad Home, End, PgUp, PgDn, Del and Enter keys
into the corresponding control keys below.
.PP
Cursor motion:
.DS
.ta 6n 18n
	\fB^B\fR	left one character (wraps to previous line)
	\fB^F\fR	right one character (wraps to next line)
	\fB^P\fR	up one line
	\fB^N\fR	down one line
	\fB^A\fR	to start of line
	\fB^E\fR	to end of line
	\fB^Z\fR	up one page
	\fB^V\fR	down one page
	\fBESC <\fR	to top of file
	\fBESC >\fR	to end of file
	\fBESC f\fR	forward one word
	\fBESC b\fR	back one word
	\fB^L\fR	recentre the cursor line
.DE
Editing:
.DS
.ta 6n 18n
	\fBBS\fR	delete left; at column 0, join with previous line
	\fBDEL, ^D\fR	delete right; at end of line, join with next line
	\fB^K\fR	kill to end of line; repeated, appends and takes
		the newline too
	\fB^W\fR	kill the selection
	\fB^Y\fR	yank (insert the last killed text)
	\fB^O\fR	open a line below the cursor
	\fB^T\fR	transpose the characters around the cursor
	\fB^G\fR	abort; drop the selection
.DE
Search and file commands:
.DS
.ta 6n 18n
	\fB^S\fR	find next (asks for a pattern the first time)
	\fBESC s\fR	new search (always asks for the pattern)
	\fB^X^C\fR	quit (confirms if the buffer is modified)
.DE
Function keys:
.DS
.ta 6n 18n
	\fBF2\fR	save (asks for a name only if untitled)
	\fBF3\fR	open a file
	\fBF4\fR	new (empty) buffer
	\fBF5\fR	cut the selection to the clipboard
	\fBF6\fR	copy the selection to the clipboard
	\fBF7\fR	paste the clipboard
	\fBF8\fR	find next
	\fBF10\fR	quit (delivered as ^X^C)
	\fBHELP\fR	show the help summary
	\fBCLR/HOME\fR	to top of file
	\fBSTOP\fR	abort; drop the selection
.DE
Printable characters and TAB insert at the cursor.
Tabs are stored literally and displayed at 8-column stops.
RETURN splits the line at the cursor.
.PP
The mouse places the cursor with a left click, and a left drag selects
text; on release the selection becomes the PRIMARY selection, which a
middle click pastes in any GUI window (at the click point).
Cut and Copy instead use the clipboard, a separate store, so a
clipboard paste and a middle-click paste can carry different text.
.PP
The window menu offers New, Open, Save, Cut, Copy, Paste and Help
above the standard window entries.
Menu Save always shows the file-name dialog, prefilled with the
current name;
.B F2
saves silently under the current name.
In dialogs, TAB cycles among text fields, RETURN fires the default
button and ESC cancels.
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 8n 32n
	/tmp/.hrclip	clipboard
	/usr/hr/icons/edit.icn	desktop icon
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR zview ,
.BR zterm ,
.BR zfile ,
.B ed
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
All diagnostics appear in dialogs:
`Cannot open that file',
`Cannot write that file',
`Enter a file name',
`Enter text to find',
`Not found',
`Discard unsaved changes?'.
.SH NOTES
The buffer holds at most 1024 lines of at most 240 characters.
Loading stops at the line limit, and longer lines are split; either
event marks the buffer modified so the truncation cannot be saved back
silently.
The kill buffer holds 2048 bytes, a search pattern 31 characters, and
a file name 39 characters.
Every line is written back with a trailing newline.
There is no undo and no replace command.
